

-> Introduction to Spring Framework

What is Spring Framework?
Spring Framework is a lightweight Java framework that helps build enterprise-level applications easily by managing:
Object creation
Dependencies
Configuration
Cross-cutting concerns (logging, security, transactions)

👉 Core idea: You focus on business logic, Spring handles infrastructure.


-> Problems in Traditional Java
   Too many new keywords
   Tight coupling between classes
   Hard to test (dependencies fixed)
   Heavy configuration (EJB earlier)
   Boilerplate code


-> How Spring Solves Them

| Problem            | Spring Solution      |
| ------------------ | -------------------- |
| Tight coupling     | Dependency Injection |
| Object creation    | IoC Container        |
| Hard testing       | Mockable beans       |
| Boilerplate        | Annotations          |
| Cross-cutting code | AOP                  |

-> Spring vs Traditional Java Development
| Traditional Java         | Spring                       |
| ------------------------ | ---------------------------- |
| Objects created manually | Objects created by container |
| Tightly coupled          | Loosely coupled              |
| Hard to test             | Easy unit testing            |
| Lots of boilerplate      | Minimal code                 |
| No lifecycle management  | Full lifecycle management    |


-> Real-World Use Cases of Spring
   Banking systems
   E-commerce applications
   REST APIs & Microservices
   Enterprise web applications
   Cloud-based systems
